# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# EstimateIQ environment variables
# The app runs with NONE of these set (local-first / zero-key):
#   - DATABASE_URL defaults to a local SQLite file
#   - the LLM falls back to a deterministic mock interviewer
#   - auth runs in dev mode
# Set values below to swap in real services. No code changes needed.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Database — local SQLite by default. Point at Postgres/Supabase in prod.
DATABASE_URL="file:./dev.db"

# ── LLM provider (optional) ──
# Leave unset to use the offline mock interviewer.
# Set LLM_PROVIDER to "gemini" | "anthropic" | "openai" | "mock".
# Omit it and the provider is auto-detected from whichever key is present,
# preferring Gemini (the only one with a free tier).
#
# Google AI Studio issues a free key with no card: https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
# GEMINI_API_KEY=""
#
# ── More than one key per provider ──
# Every provider below reads a numbered ROTATION, not a single key:
#   GEMINI_API_KEY, GEMINI_API_KEY_2, GEMINI_API_KEY_3, … up to _10
# and the same for ANTHROPIC_, OPENAI_ and NVIDIA_. Slot one keeps the plain name,
# so a deployment with one key is simply a one-item rotation and needs no change.
#
# A key that is rate limited, out of quota or rejected hands the turn to the next
# key in ITS OWN provider before the chain falls through to LLM_FALLBACK_PROVIDER
# — a second Gemini key is a better answer than a weaker second engine. Only once
# every key is spent does the mock take over.
#
# This is worth doing because the ceiling below is PER KEY and shared by everyone
# on the deployment: one key is one cohort's afternoon, three is three.
#
# Gaps are skipped rather than read as the end of the list, and a key pasted into
# two slots is counted once.
#
# WHERE TO PUT THEM: here, in `.env` (already gitignored), and in your host's
# environment settings for production. Not in a notes file or a document beside
# the code — `.gitignore` covers `.env`, and nothing else, so a keys.txt is one
# `git add .` away from being in the repository's history permanently. A
# read-only host like Vercel has no such file to read anyway.
#
# Keys can also be added at /admin, which needs no redeploy — see AUTH_SECRET
# below. Stored keys REPLACE the variables here rather than merging with them.
#
# One thing the code cannot settle for you: whether stacking free-tier keys from
# several accounts to multiply one project's quota is allowed. Google's free tier
# is metered per project, so check their terms before relying on it. Keys that are
# genuinely separate — a paid key behind a free one, or different departments'
# billing projects — raise no such question.
#
# Optional model override. Default is gemini-2.5-flash.
# Free-tier ceilings are per key and shared by ALL users of the deployment:
#   gemini-2.5-flash        250 requests/day   (default — best instruction-following)
#   gemini-2.5-flash-lite  1000 requests/day   (4x the headroom, somewhat weaker)
# Tune lib/config/practice.ts -> llmBudget to match whichever you pick.
# LLM_MODEL="gemini-2.5-flash"
#
# Paid alternatives — no free tier on either.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=""
# OPENAI_API_KEY=""

# ── NVIDIA NIM (optional) ──
# The hosted catalogue at build.nvidia.com. OpenAI-compatible, streaming, and
# metered — the spend guards in lib/config/practice.ts apply to it exactly as
# they do to Gemini.
#
# Setting this key makes NVIDIA the auto-detected provider, AHEAD of Gemini.
# Set LLM_PROVIDER explicitly if you keep more than one key on the same machine.
# NVIDIA_API_KEY=""
#
# The model id is the namespaced one from the model's page (publisher/name).
# Kept separate from LLM_MODEL, which is shared by every provider, so a name
# left over from another one is never sent here. Default: meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct
# NVIDIA_MODEL="meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct"

# ── Local model for development (optional) ──
# Runs the interviewer against Ollama on your own machine: free, unmetered and
# offline, so you can iterate without spending the shared Gemini quota.
#   1. install Ollama. On Windows/macOS it already runs in the tray; on Linux: ollama serve
#   2. ollama pull qwen2.5:7b
#   3. LLM_PROVIDER=ollama   (it is never auto-detected — there is no key to find)
# LLM_PROVIDER="ollama"
#
# Model to run. Kept separate from LLM_MODEL, which is shared by every provider,
# so a leftover gemini-2.5-flash isn't sent to Ollama. Default is qwen2.5:7b;
# llama3.2 is much faster on a machine without a GPU, and noticeably weaker.
# OLLAMA_MODEL="qwen2.5:7b"
#
# Any OpenAI-compatible local server works here (LM Studio, vLLM, …).
# OLLAMA_BASE_URL="http://localhost:11434/v1"

# ── Second engine, when the first can't answer (optional) ──
# A turn that the provider above cannot serve — key rejected, credits gone, rate
# limited twice, unreachable, or a 200 that streams nothing back — is handed to
# this one instead of dropping to the offline mock. The mock is still the floor
# if both fail, and a reply already streaming is never finished by the other
# engine: it is left truncated rather than changing voice mid-paragraph.
#
# Opt-in by name, like LLM_PROVIDER=ollama and for the same reason: nothing here
# is sniffed, so no stray env var can route real turns to a localhost model that
# isn't running. Pointing it at a server that is down only costs one failed
# request before the mock.
#
# The pairing this exists for — hosted NVIDIA in front, local Ollama behind:
#   LLM_PROVIDER=nvidia
#   LLM_FALLBACK_PROVIDER=ollama
# Any provider name works on either side. Naming the same provider twice, or the
# mock, is ignored: neither adds a link the chain doesn't already have.
# LLM_FALLBACK_PROVIDER="ollama"

# ── Auth session secret (optional; a dev default is used if unset) ──
# Also the key that API keys stored at /admin are encrypted under, which is why
# the panel refuses to save one until this is set to something real. The shipped
# default is published in this repository, so encrypting under it would only look
# like protection. Until then the app reads the numbered variables above instead.
# AUTH_SECRET=""

# ── Avatar storage (optional) ──
# Where uploaded profile photos are kept. Unset means "db": the image goes in
# the database the app already has, which is why avatars work on a fresh clone
# and on a read-only filesystem like Vercel's, with no bucket and no keys.
# Adding an object store is a file in lib/storage/ and a case in its index —
# see the checklist in lib/storage/index.ts.
# AVATAR_STORE="db"

# ── Deferred integrations (config stubs; not required to run) ──
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY=""
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST="https://app.posthog.com"
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=""
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""
# NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=""
